\f0\fs24 \cf0 How the OME85 Test Code Works with ESP32-S3 Dev Board\
\
Direct Register Control: Standard Arduino functions like digitalWrite() add overhead. The code accesses raw ESP32-S3 registers (GPIO_OUT_W1TS_REG, GPIO_OUT_W1TC_REG, and GPIO_IN_REG) to toggle clock pins and read data bits within nanoseconds, achieving stable ~500 kHz bit-banging.\
\
Atomic Framing (noInterrupts): FreeRTOS constantly pauses code to manage Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, and system background tasks. Shielding the entire BiSS-C handshake and frame capture inside noInterrupts() prevents CPU context-switching from causing frame timeouts.\
\
Propagated Edge Stabilization: Level shifters (like the TXS0108E) introduce microsecond rise-time delays. The inline sampleSettleDelay() function inserts precise CPU NOP (no-operation) instruction cycles after forcing the clock HIGH, giving signal lines time to settle before reading the bit state.\
\
Frame Demodulation & Verification: The state machine polls for the encoder's ACK (LOW) and Start (HIGH) transitions, ignores the CDS bit, and streams in 17 position bits, 2 Error/Warning (EW) status bits, and 6 CRC bits. It verifies frame integrity using a linear feedback shift register with polynomial 0x43.\
\
Modifications & Customization Tuning\
\
1. Encoder Hardware Variations\
\
Resolution Settings: If switching to a different encoder model (e.g., 14-bit, 18-bit, or 20-bit), update DATA_BITS and adjust the position bitmask (positionData & 0xFFFF). For example, a 14-bit encoder requires DATA_BITS = 15 and mask 0x3FFF.\
\
CRC Polynomial: Standard BiSS-C defaults to 6-bit polynomial 0x43. If using an encoder vendor with proprietary CRC logic (e.g., 0x03 or 0x1D), update CRC_POLY and CRC_BITS.\
\
2. Speed and Noise Optimization\
\
Clock Frequency: Adjust clockPhaseDelay() NOP counts down to increase transaction speeds, or increase NOPs if using long cables susceptible to high-frequency signal attenuation.\
\
Signal Settle Window: If replacing the level shifter with fast line receivers (like an SN75176 or MAX3096 RS-422 transceiver), sampleSettleDelay() can be reduced to single-digit NOPs for higher bandwidth.\
\
3. Integration into Real-Time Control Loops\
\
High-Frequency Motion Loops: Remove Serial.print() outputs and the delay(2) call to run the reader inside a high-speed PID control loop, allowing position updates up to 5 kHz.\
\
GPIO Pin Reassignment: If changing pin assignments, choose pins between GPIO 0 and 31. Pins above 31 use the secondary register bank (GPIO_OUT1_W1TS_REG), requiring updated macro definitions.}